Design Browser History

(Stack / Doubly Linked List Design Problem)

🟢 Problem Statement

Design a browser history system that supports:

BrowserHistory(String homepage)
void visit(String url)
String back(int steps)
String forward(int steps)

Rules:

Browser starts at homepage

visit(url):

Clears forward history

Moves to new URL

back(steps):

Move back at most steps

forward(steps):

Move forward at most steps

🟢 Example
Input:
BrowserHistory("google.com")
visit("facebook.com")
visit("youtube.com")
back(1)
back(1)
forward(1)
visit("linkedin.com")
forward(2)
back(2)
back(7)

Output:
"facebook.com"
"google.com"
"facebook.com"
"linkedin.com"
"google.com"

🟢 Approach 1 (Simple & Clean)

Use:

ArrayList to store history

Pointer to track current position

This is simpler and preferred in interviews.

🟢 Data Structures
Structure	Purpose
ArrayList	Store pages
int current	Current index
🟢 Algorithm
visit(url)

Remove all elements after current index

Add new url

Move pointer

back(steps)
current = max(0, current - steps)


Return history[current]

forward(steps)
current = min(size-1, current + steps)


Return history[current]

🟢 Java Code (Interview Clean Version)
import java.util.*;

class BrowserHistory {

    List<String> history;
    int current;

    public BrowserHistory(String homepage) {
        history = new ArrayList<>();
        history.add(homepage);
        current = 0;
    }

    public void visit(String url) {
        // Remove forward history
        while(history.size() > current + 1) {
            history.remove(history.size() - 1);
        }

        history.add(url);
        current++;
    }

    public String back(int steps) {
        current = Math.max(0, current - steps);
        return history.get(current);
    }

    public String forward(int steps) {
        current = Math.min(history.size() - 1, current + steps);
        return history.get(current);
    }
}

🟢 Line-by-Line Explanation
Constructor
history = new ArrayList<>();
history.add(homepage);
current = 0;


Initialize with homepage at index 0.

visit(url)
while(history.size() > current + 1)
    history.remove(last element);


⚠ Important:

If we previously went back and now visit new page → forward history must be deleted.

Then:

history.add(url);
current++;


Move pointer to new page.

back(steps)
current = Math.max(0, current - steps);


We cannot go below index 0.

forward(steps)
current = Math.min(history.size()-1, current + steps);


Cannot exceed last index.

